  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows a deepening upper low \nalmost right over northeast Kansas. Just ahead of the southward \nmoving low, strong upward motion is supporting a few flurries. \nCurrent radar suggests that a few flakes may be reaching the ground \nin spots west and south of Manhattan, but little more than that. \nCould see a few more flurries in far eastern KS tomorrow morning as \nthe deformation snow band to our east pivots back west, but again \nnot expecting any impacts to holiday travel across our area. Chilly \nair will remain the main impact, with highs in the low 40s today and \ntomorrow and overnight lows in the low 20s.\n\nStill expecting a warming trend into the 50s for Friday and \nSaturday. The upper low pulls off to the east and shortwave ridging \nmoves in ahead of the next system, allowing southerly flow to \nreturn. By Saturday night into Sunday morning, guidance has become \nmore consistent in a deeper upper trough, supporting a fairly deep \n(~1000 mb) surface low that will move across Oklahoma and into the \nOzarks. Increasing frontogenesis should support a deformation band \nof precipitation that will move across the area, on the northwest \nside of the low. Low-level cold air initially looks to be limited, \nso still expecting most precipitation to be a cold steady rain. As \nprecipitation ends, 850 mb temps may cool just enough for a few wet \nsnowflakes to mix in - with the ECMWF and its ensembles most bullish \non this potential. Even in this scenario though, accumulation isn't \nexpected, with only around 10% of ensembles suggesting even light \naccumulations on grassy surfaces.\n\nAn active split-flow pattern continues into next week. Temperatures \nremain near average overall, with some day-to-day fluctuations as \nanother couple systems approach. The first one looks to be rather \nmoisture starved as it passes through Monday into Tuesday, with \nmedium confidence in a deeper upper trough near the end of the \nforecast period mid next week.",
